The filesystem hierarchy in Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8 (RHEL 8) organizes all files and directories in a standardized structure. It follows the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS) to ensure consistency. Everything starts from the root directory (/) and branches into other directories.
/          – Root directory
/bin       – Essential user commands (ls, cp, mv)
/boot      – Bootloader files, kernel (vmlinuz, grub)
/dev       – Device files (disks, USB devices)
/etc       – System configuration files (network, users)
/home      – User home directories (e.g., /home/user1)
/root      – Home directory for root user
/lib /lib64 – Essential shared libraries
/media     – Auto-mounted external devices
/mnt       – Manually mounted devices
/opt       – Optional or third-party software
/proc      – Virtual filesystem (system/process info)
/sys       – Kernel and hardware information
/tmp       – Temporary files (cleared on reboot)
/usr       – User programs and utilities
    /usr/bin   – User commands
    /usr/lib   – Libraries
    /usr/share – Shared data
/var       – Variable data (logs, databases, mail)
    /var/log   – Log files
    /var/spool – Spool files
/run       – Runtime data (process IDs, system info)

