File Descriptors in Linux

A file descriptor is a number that represents an open file or I/O stream for a process. Linux assigns these numbers by default for standard streams:

FD	Name	Meaning / Purpose	Default Source/Destination
0	stdin	Standard input	Keyboard or redirected file
1	stdout	Standard output	Terminal or redirected file
