File Descriptors in Linux

A file descriptor is a number that represents an open file or I/O stream for a process. Linux assigns these numbers by default for standard streams:

FD	Name	Meaning / Purpose	Default Source/Destination
0	stdin	Standard input	Keyboard or redirected file
1	stdout	Standard output	Terminal or redirected file
2	stderr	Standard error	Terminal or redirected file

Explanation
0 = stdin → input to a program (e.g., keyboard or piped input)
1 = stdout → normal output from a program (e.g., messages, command output)
2 = stderr → error messages from a program

These numbers let Linux distinguish between normal output and errors and allow redirection like:
ls /nonexistent > out.txt 2> err.txt
> redirects FD 1 (stdout)
2> redirects FD 2 (stderr)

💡 Tip:
Think of file descriptors as channels:
0 → keyboard/input
1 → screen/normal output
2 → screen/error output

3️⃣ Ways to Change File Permissions
Method 1️⃣: Symbolic (letters)
chmod u+rwx,g+rx,o-r file.txt
u = owner
g = group
o = others
+ add permission, - remove, = set exact
Example:
chmod g+w file.txt   # give write permission to group
Method 2️⃣: Numeric / Octal
Numeric codes:
r = 4
w = 2
x = 1
Add them for each category (Owner | Group | Others):
chmod 754 file.txt
7 = 4+2+1 → owner rwx
5 = 4+0+1 → group r-x
4 = 4+0+0 → others r--
1️⃣ Changing Ownership of a File or Directory
Linux uses chown to change owner and group.
Change owner of a file
sudo chown newowner filename
Example:
sudo chown john file.txt
✅ Now john is the owner of file.txt.
Change group of a file
sudo chgrp groupname filename
Example:
sudo chgrp developers file.txt
✅ Now the group of file.txt is developers.
Change both owner and group
sudo chown john:developers file.txt
✅ john is owner and developers is group.
🔷 2️⃣ Changing Ownership of Directories Recursively
To change all files and subdirectories under a directory:
sudo chown -R john:developers /path/to/directory
-R = recursive
Applies to all files and subdirectories
🔷 3️⃣ Giving Permissions to a Directory and Its Contents
Linux uses chmod for permissions.
Change permissions of a directory
chmod 755 /path/to/directory
7 → owner: rwx
5 → group: r-x
5 → others: r-x
Recursive permission change
chmod -R 755 /path/to/directory
✅ Now all files and subdirectories inside get the same permission.

What is Setuid?

Setuid stands for “Set User ID”.
It is a special permission on executable files.
When a file has setuid, anyone who runs the file will execute it with the file owner’s permissions, not their own.

Think of it like this:
You normally run a program as your user. With setuid, you temporarily “borrow” the file owner’s powers to run the program.

🔷 2️⃣ Why is Setuid Useful?
Some programs need higher privileges to do their job, but you don’t want to give everyone full access to the system.

Common Use Cases:
passwd command
Updates /etc/shadow (password file)
Must run as root to write password data
Normal users can execute it safely because setuid is set
Custom programs
Script or binary that needs to access restricted files
Users run it safely without giving full root privileges

🔷 3️⃣ How to Check if Setuid is Set
Use ls -l on the executable:
ls -l /usr/bin/passwd
Output example:
-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root 54256 Mar 23 12:00 /usr/bin/passwd
Notice:
s in the owner execute position (rws) → setuid is set
Owner: root
Anyone running it executes as root temporarily

🔷 4️⃣ How to Set Setuid
Syntax:
chmod u+s filename
Example:
sudo chmod u+s myscript.sh
u+s → add setuid to owner
ls -l myscript.sh → -rwsr-xr-x

Remove Setuid:
chmod u-s myscript.sh

Numeric Way
Add 4 as the first digit of chmod for setuid:
chmod 4755 myscript.sh
4 → setuid
7 → owner rwx
5 → group r-x
5 → others r-x

What is Setgid?

Setgid stands for “Set Group ID”.
It is a special permission that can be applied to files or directories.

Behavior
On files:
Executes the file with the file’s group permissions instead of the user’s group.
Useful for programs that need group-level privileges.
On directories:
Any new file or folder created inside inherits the directory’s group automatically.
Makes collaboration easier, especially in team/shared directories.

🔷 2️⃣ Why is Setgid Used?
To ensure consistency in group ownership for new files in shared directories
To allow users to execute a file with the file’s group privileges
To control access in multi-user environments

Example:
A team project directory /project should have all new files owned by group developers, no matter who creates them.

🔷 3️⃣ How to Check Setgid
Use ls -l:
ls -ld /project
Output example:
drwxrwsr-x 2 john developers 4096 Mar 23 12:00 /project
Notice:
s in group execute position (rws) → setgid is set
Without setgid, it would show rwx

🔷 4️⃣ How to Set Setgid
Syntax (Symbolic method)
chmod g+s filename_or_directory
g+s → add setgid
Symbolic method preserves existing permissions ✅

Example:
sudo chmod g+s /project
Now /project is a setgid directory
New files created inside will inherit the group developers

Remove Setgid
chmod g-s filename_or_directory

Numeric Method
First digit controls special permissions:
4 → setuid
2 → setgid
1 → sticky bit

Example:
Current permissions of directory: rwxr-xr-x → 755
Add setgid: 2 + 755 → 2755
chmod 2755 /project

Check:
ls -ld /project
drwxrwsr-x 2 john developers 4096 Mar 23 12:00 /project

What is Sticky Bit?

Sticky bit is a special permission used only on directories (mostly).
It prevents users from deleting or renaming files they don’t own, even if they have write permission to the directory.

Think of it like a “locked playground”:
Everyone can play/write, but only the owner of a toy (file) can take it away.

🔷 2️⃣ Why Sticky Bit is Used
Shared directories where multiple users can create files
Prevents accidental or malicious deletion of other users’ files
Common in /tmp, /var/tmp, or team project directories

🔷 3️⃣ How to Check Sticky Bit
Use ls -ld:
ls -ld /tmp
Example output:
drwxrwxrwt 10 root root 4096 Mar 23 12:00 /tmp
Notice:
t at the end of permissions → sticky bit is set
Normal permissions: drwxrwxrw- → sticky bit adds t at the end

🔷 4️⃣ How to Set Sticky Bit
Symbolic Method
chmod +t /directory
Example:
sudo chmod +t /shared
ls -ld /shared
drwxrwxrwt 2 john developers 4096 Mar 23 12:00 /shared
✅ Now, only file owners can delete their own files in /shared.

Remove Sticky Bit
chmod -t /directory

Numeric Method
Sticky bit = 1 in first digit of numeric mode
Example:
chmod 1777 /shared
1 → sticky bit
7 → owner rwx
7 → group rwx
7 → others rwx
Check with ls -ld /shared → drwxrwxrwt

🔷 5️⃣ Practical Use Cases
/tmp Directory
Shared by all users
