Step 1: Default File Creation
In Linux:
When you create a new file, the system starts with default permissions:
Files: 666 → rw-rw-rw-
Directories: 777 → rwxrwxrwx
Note: Files do not get execute (x) by default.
1️⃣ What is umask?
umask stands for User File Creation Mask.
It defines the default permissions for new files and directories when they are created.
Think of it as a “permission filter”: it removes permissions from the default set.
🔷 2️⃣ How umask Works
Default permissions before umask:
Type	Default Permission
File	666 → rw-rw-rw-
Directory	777 → rwxrwxrwx
umask subtracts permissions from these defaults.

Formula:

Default permission – umask = final permission
Example:
umask 022
Default file permission: 666 → rw-rw-rw-
Subtract umask 022:
Owner: 6 – 0 = 6 → rw-
Group: 6 – 2 = 4 → r--
Others: 6 – 2 = 4 → r--
✅ New file permissions: rw-r--r--
Default directory permission: 777 → rwxrwxrwx
Subtract umask 022 → rwxr-xr-x
🔷 3️⃣ Check Current umask
umask

Output example:

0022
🔷 4️⃣ Set Temporary umask
umask 007
New files → owner full, group full, others none
New directories → owner rwx, group rwx, others none
🔷 5️⃣ Permanent umask
Set umask in shell configuration files:
Shell	File
Bash	~/.bashrc or ~/.bash_profile
Zsh	~/.zshrc

Example:

echo "umask 027" >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc
Now default file creation uses this mask every login
🔷 6️⃣ Numeric vs Symbolic
Numeric: umask 022
Symbolic: umask u=rwx,g=rx,o=

1️⃣ What are ACLs?

ACLs (Access Control Lists) are an advanced way to manage file and directory permissions in Linux.
They allow you to give different permissions to multiple users or groups on a single file or directory.
Traditional permissions (owner, group, others) are limited, but ACLs are more flexible.

Example:
Normal permission: rw-r--r-- → owner can read/write, group can read, others can read
ACL: user:alice:rw- → now Alice can also read/write even if she’s not the owner or in the group

🔷 2️⃣ Why Use ACLs?
When multiple users need different levels of access to a file or directory
When group-based permissions are not enough
Fine-grained access control without changing ownership

Use Cases:
Project directories shared among multiple users
Restrict access to sensitive files for certain users
Allow temporary access to a file for a user without changing group ownership

🔷 3️⃣ Check if a File/Directory Supports ACL
Most modern Linux systems support ACL by default. You can check with:
mount | grep acl
Or check a file:
getfacl filename

🔷 4️⃣ Viewing ACLs
Command: getfacl
getfacl file.txt

Example Output:
# file: file.txt
# owner: john
# group: developers
user::rw-
user:alice:r--
group::r--
mask::rw-
other::r--
user:alice:r-- → Alice has read permission
mask → maximum permissions allowed for users/groups other than owner
other → everyone else

🔷 5️⃣ Setting ACLs
Add permission for a specific user
setfacl -m u:alice:rw file.txt
-m → modify
u:alice:rw → give Alice read/write access

Add permission for a group
setfacl -m g:qa:r file.txt
g:qa:r → group qa gets read permission

Remove ACL entry
setfacl -x u:alice file.txt
Removes Alice’s ACL permissions

Set default ACL for directories
So all new files inherit ACL:
setfacl -d -m u:alice:rw /project
-d → default ACL for new files
All new files created in /project → Alice automatically gets read/write

🔷 6️⃣ Recursive ACLs
Apply ACLs to all files and subdirectories:
setfacl -R -m u:alice:rw /project
-R → recursivel

What is a Hard Link?
A hard link is another name for the same file data on disk.
In Linux, a file is stored using something called an inode (which holds the actual data). A hard link just creates another filename pointing to the same inode.
👉 So both names are equal — there is no “original” vs “copy.”
🛠️ How to Create a Hard Link
Use the ln command:
ln file1.txt file2.txt
Now:
file1.txt and file2.txt are hard links to the same file
🔍 How to Check It
ls -li
You’ll see:
Same inode number
Link count increases (e.g., 2 instead of 1)
⚙️ How to Use a Hard Link
You use it just like a normal file:
echo "Hello" >> file1.txt
cat file2.txt
👉 Output will show "Hello" because both are the same data.
You can:
Read from either
Write to either
Delete either (data still exists if one link remains)
📦 Real-Life Uses
Hard links are used when you want:
1. Backup without duplication
Create “copies” of files without using extra disk space
2. File versioning
Keep multiple names for the same file state
3. System efficiency
Linux sometimes uses hard links internally to save space
❓ Does It Use Extra Space?
👉 No (almost zero extra space)
It does NOT duplicate file data
Only a tiny amount of space is used for the new filename entry
✔ Example:
1 GB file → create 5 hard links → still ~1 GB total (not 5 GB)
⚠️ What Happens When You Delete Files?
Case 1: Delete one link
rm file1.txt
👉 File still exists via file2.txt
Case 2: Delete all links
rm file2.txt
👉 Now data is actually deleted

What is a Soft Link (Symbolic Link)?
A soft link (symlink) is like a shortcut that points to another file or directory.
👉 It does not store the data, it stores the path to the original file.
🛠️ How to Create a Soft Link
Use ln -s:
ln -s original.txt symlink.txt
Now:
symlink.txt → points to original.txt
🔍 How to Identify a Soft Link
ls -l
Output looks like:
symlink.txt -> original.txt
👉 Key signs:
Starts with l (like lrwxrwxrwx)
Shows -> pointing to target
⚙️ Why Are Soft Links Used?
1. Shortcut / Easy access
Access long paths quickly
2. Link directories
Unlike hard links, symlinks can point to folders
3. Cross-filesystem linking
Works across different disks/partitions
4. System management
Linux uses them for paths like /bin, /lib, etc.
📦 Do Soft Links Use Extra Space?
👉 Yes, but very little
They store only the file path
Size is usually just a few bytes
✔ Example:
1 GB file → symlink → only a few bytes extra
❓ What Happens If File Is Deleted?
Case 1: Delete original file
rm original.txt
👉 Result:
symlink.txt becomes broken (dangling link)
cat symlink.txt
❌ Error: No such file
Case 2: Delete symlink
rm symlink.txt
👉 Result:
Original file is NOT affected

What is an Inode?
An inode (index node) is a data structure in Linux that stores information about a file.
👉 It does NOT store the file name or actual data, but it stores metadata like:
File size
Permissions (read/write/execute)
Owner (user & group)
Timestamps (created, modified, accessed)
Number of links
Pointer to actual data blocks
📦 Simple Idea
Filename → points to → inode
inode → points to → actual data on disk
👉 So:
File name is just a label, inode is the real identity of the file
🔍 How to View Inodes
1. Show inode numbers
ls -li
Example output:
123456 -rw-r--r-- 2 user user 100 Mar 24 file1.txt
123456 -rw-r--r-- 2 user user 100 Mar 24 file2.txt
👉 Both files have same inode → they are hard links
2. Detailed inode info
stat file1.txt
Shows:
inode number
size
permissions
timestamps
⚙️ How Inodes Are Used
1. File identification
Linux uses inode number to identify files internally
2. Hard links
Multiple filenames → same inode
That’s why hard links share data
3. File deletion
File is deleted only when inode link count = 0
🧪 Example to Understand
echo "Hello" > file1.txt
ln file1.txt file2.txt
ls -li
👉 Output:
Same inode number
Link count = 2
Now delete one:
rm file1.txt
👉 inode still exists → data still accessible via file2.txt

1. Understanding Users in RHEL
In Linux, every person or service that can access the system has a user account.
Each user has a username and user ID (UID).
There is also a special user called root, which has full administrative privileges.
You can list users in RHEL by checking /etc/passwd:
cat /etc/passwd
2. Basic Command to Switch Users: su
The su command (short for substitute user or switch user) allows you to switch from your current user to another user account.
Syntax:
su [options] [username]
If no username is provided, su defaults to root.
You will be prompted for the password of the user you are switching to.
Examples:
Switch to root user:
su -
- ensures you get root’s environment variables, not just access.
You’ll be asked for the root password.
Switch to another user, e.g., john:
su - john
You’ll need to know john’s password.
The - ensures you load the new user’s environment.
Switch without loading environment:
su john
This keeps your current environment variables.
Useful if you just want access but not the full login environment.
3. Switching Users with sudo
On RHEL, direct root login may be disabled for security. Instead, sudo is used to execute commands as another user.
Syntax:
sudo -i          # Switch to root with full environment
sudo -u username command   # Run command as another user
Examples:
Switch to root:
sudo -i
You’ll enter your own password, not root’s.
Run a command as another user:
sudo -u john whoami
This executes whoami as user john.
Useful for scripts or single commands without fully logging in.
4. Check Current User
Sometimes you want to verify which user you are:
whoami
or
id
whoami → shows current username
id → shows UID, GID, and groups

1. What an Environment Includes
An environment typically contains:
Environment variables – key-value pairs that control behavior of programs.
Examples:
PATH → directories where the system looks for commands.
HOME → your home directory.
USER → your username.
LANG → language/locale settings.
Shell settings – configuration like prompt style, aliases, history settings.
Program-specific settings – some applications read environment variables to decide how to behave.
2. Why Environment Matters
When you switch users, the environment determines things like:
Which PATH directories are used for commands.
Which default editor opens when you type nano or vi.
Which language or locale is active.
Example:
echo $PATH
Shows directories the shell searches for commands.
If you switch users with su -, the PATH is updated to the new user’s environment.
If you switch without -, the PATH may remain as your old user, causing confusion.
3. Checking Your Environment
You can see all environment variables using:
printenv
or
env
Example output:
USER=alice
HOME=/home/alice
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
LANG=en_US.UTF-8
4. Environment Files
Each user has configuration files that set up the environment when they log in:
~/.bash_profile → runs when you log in via terminal.
~/.bashrc → runs for interactive shells.
/etc/profile → system-wide environment settings.
These files define things like aliases, prompt, PATH, and other variables.

1. Foreground Process
Definition:
A foreground process is a process that runs directly in the terminal you started it from and blocks the terminal until it finishes. You cannot use that terminal for anything else until the process completes.
How to identify:
Usually, you just start a command, and it runs directly.
You can see it with the ps command:
ps -f
In top, it will show up with the terminal associated in the TTY column.
How to run a program as foreground:
Simply type the command normally:
gedit filename.txt
The terminal waits until you close gedit before returning to the prompt.
Meaning in practice:
Useful when you want to interactively work with the program.
Example: editing a file, running a script, compiling code.
2. Background Process
Definition:
A background process runs independently of the terminal. You can continue using the terminal while the process runs.
How to identify:
Use jobs to list jobs in the current terminal.
jobs
Use ps or top to see all running processes:
ps -ef | grep process_name
In top, the TTY column might show ? if it’s fully detached.
How to run a program in the background:
Add & at the end of the command:
sleep 60 &
You can move a running foreground process to background:
Press Ctrl+Z to pause it.
Then use bg to resume it in the background:
bg %1
Meaning in practice:
Useful for long-running tasks like downloading files, running scripts, or compiling code without blocking the terminal.
3. Daemon
Definition:
A daemon is a background service that usually starts automatically and does not interact with any terminal. Examples include web servers, database servers, cron jobs, and SSH daemons.
How to identify:
Usually runs with root or service-specific users.
ps -ef | grep daemon_name
systemctl status service_name (preferred in RHEL 7+)
systemctl status httpd
How to run a daemon/service:
Start using systemctl:
sudo systemctl start httpd
Enable to start at boot:
sudo systemctl enable httpd
Stop or check status:
sudo systemctl stop httpd
sudo systemctl status httpd
Meaning in practice:
Daemons run silently in the background and provide system services.
They are crucial for servers and automated tasks.
No terminal interaction is required or expected.

1. Running (R)
Definition: The process is actively executing on the CPU or ready to run.
How to identify:
ps aux
Look at the STAT column — R means running.
In top, running processes usually appear at the top of the list.
Example:
top
firefox actively using CPU → Running (R)
Meaning in practice:
The process is doing work right now.
Multiple processes may be ready to run; the OS schedules them on the CPU.
2. Sleeping (S)
Definition: The process is waiting for an event — usually input/output (I/O) like disk, network, or keyboard input.
How to identify:
ps aux
STAT column shows S → sleeping (interruptible)
STAT = D → uninterruptible sleep (usually waiting for disk or I/O)
Example:
sleep 60
The sleep process is in sleeping state until time is up.
Meaning in practice:
Sleeping processes don’t use CPU until the event occurs.
Most processes spend a lot of time sleeping, waiting for user input or data.
3. Stopped (T)
Definition: The process has been paused — usually by a signal (like Ctrl+Z) or via kill -STOP.
How to identify:
ps aux
STAT = T → stopped
Example:
sleep 100
# Press Ctrl+Z
Process stops → you can see it in jobs:
jobs
[1]+  Stopped  sleep 100
Meaning in practice:
Useful for temporarily pausing a process.
You can resume it with:
bg   # continue in background
fg   # continue in foreground
4. Zombie (Z)
Definition: The process has finished execution, but its parent process hasn’t read its exit status.
How to identify:
ps aux
STAT = Z → zombie
Often shows <defunct> in the process name.
Example:
# A child process exits, parent doesn't call wait()
ps aux | grep defunct
Meaning in practice:
Zombies don’t use CPU or memory for execution.
They occupy a process table entry.
Usually removed when the parent exits or reaps the child.
Large numbers of zombies can indicate a poorly written program.


Using ps Command
The ps command shows running processes and who owns them.
Command:
ps aux
USER       PID %CPU %MEM    VSZ   RSS TTY      STAT START   TIME COMMAND
root         1  0.0  0.1  22592  4092 ?        Ss   10:00   0:02 /usr/lib/systemd/systemd
alice     2345  0.1  1.2  50000 12000 pts/0    S    10:05   0:01 bash
bob       3456  5.0  2.0 200000 50000 pts/1    R    10:06   0:10 firefox

1. Viewing Processes
ps command – static snapshot of processes:
ps aux      # all processes with details
ps -u alice # processes for user alice
ps -ef      # full-format for all processes
top / htop – real-time monitoring:
top         # live CPU/memory view
top -u alice # live view for a specific user
htop        # interactive, can kill/sort/filter
2. Stopping and Suspending Processes
Ctrl + Z – stop the foreground process and put it in stopped state:
sleep 100
# Press Ctrl+Z
[1]+  Stopped  sleep 100
jobs – list your stopped or background jobs:
jobs
kill -STOP <PID> – stop any process (sends STOP signal):
kill -STOP 2345
3. Moving Processes to Background
When you start a process in foreground, you can move it to background:
Using & when starting:
sleep 100 &
Move a stopped job to background:
bg %1   # resumes job 1 in background
Check background jobs:
jobs
4. Bringing Processes to Foreground
Bring a background or stopped job back to foreground:
fg %1   # brings job 1 to foreground
Useful if you want to interact with it again.
5. Killing Processes
Normal users can kill their own processes:
kill <PID>       # sends SIGTERM (polite)
kill -9 <PID>    # sends SIGKILL (force)
Root or sudo users can kill anyone’s process:
sudo kill -9 <PID>
sudo pkill chrome # kills all chrome processes
Using top or htop to kill:
top → press k → enter PID → signal
htop → select process → F9 → choose signal

What %1, %2 mean
%1, %2, etc., are job identifiers in the shell.
Each job you start in the current terminal session gets a job number, starting from 1.
They are not PIDs — they are just references used by the shell to manage jobs.
2. How jobs get numbered
Start a process in background:
sleep 100 &
Output:
[1] 2345
[1] → job number 1
2345 → PID
Start another process:
sleep 200 &
Output:
[2] 2346
[2] → job number 2
3. See current jobs
jobs

Output:

[1]-  Running                 sleep 100 &
[2]+  Running                 sleep 200 &
[1]- → job 1, last job marked -
[2]+ → job 2, last job marked + (default for fg or bg)

sleep 100 &
sleep 200 &
jobs
fg %1      # brings first sleep to foreground
bg %2      # resumes second sleep in background
kill %1    # kills the first job

1. Where you see + and -
When you run:
jobs
You might see output like this:
[1]-  Running    sleep 100 &
[2]+  Running    sleep 200 &
[1]- → job 1, marked -
[2]+ → job 2, marked +
2. What + and - mean
+ (plus) → default job
This is the most recent job.
Commands like fg or bg without any number will operate on this job.
- (minus) → previous job
This is the second most recent job.
Can be used explicitly with %<job number> if needed.
Other jobs → have no symbol; only + and - are specially marked.
3. Examples
sleep 100 &
sleep 200 &
sleep 300 &
jobs
Output might be:
[1]-  Running    sleep 100 &
[2]+  Running    sleep 200 &
[3]   Running    sleep 300 &
fg → brings job 2 (the + job) to foreground
fg %1 → brings job 1 to foreground
fg %- → brings job 1 (the - job) to foreground
bg → resumes job 2 in background by default

