1. What are signals?
A signal is a software message sent to a process to notify it of an event or command.
Signals can ask a process to stop, continue, terminate, or handle a custom action.
Every process has a PID, and you can send signals to it using kill, pkill, or killall.
2. Commonly Used Signals
Signal	Number	Purpose
SIGTERM	15	Terminate politely. The process can catch it and clean up before exiting. (default for kill <PID>).
SIGKILL	9	Force kill. Cannot be caught or ignored. Process stops immediately (kill -9 <PID>).
SIGSTOP	19	Pause the process. Cannot be ignored. (kill -STOP <PID>).
SIGCONT	18	Resume a stopped process. (kill -CONT <PID>).
SIGHUP	1	Hang up. Often tells daemons to reload configuration.
SIGINT	2	Interrupt (Ctrl+C in terminal).
SIGTSTP	20	Stop typed at terminal (Ctrl+Z).
3. Sending signals
Using kill:
kill -SIGTERM 2345   # politely terminate PID 2345
kill -9 2345         # force kill PID 2345
kill -SIGSTOP 2345   # stop/pause PID 2345
kill -SIGCONT 2345   # resume PID 2345
Using pkill by process name:
pkill -SIGTERM firefox   # politely terminate all Firefox processes
pkill -9 firefox         # force kill all Firefox processes
Using killall by process name:
killall -9 firefox
4. How signals relate to process states
Signal	Effect on STAT column in ps or top
SIGSTOP	S or T → process becomes stopped
SIGCONT	T → process resumes → R or S (running or sleeping)
SIGTERM	Process begins termination, removed from table after cleanup
SIGKILL	Process terminates immediately, removed from table
SIGINT	Foreground process stopped by Ctrl+C
5. Interactive terminal shortcuts (signals)
Key	Signal	Description
Ctrl+C	SIGINT	Stop foreground process
Ctrl+Z	SIGTSTP	Pause (stop) foreground process
fg / bg	SIGCONT	Resume stopped process

What is "nice"?
nice is used to start a process with a specific priority.
Linux processes have a priority called “nice value”:
Range: -20 (highest priority) → 19 (lowest priority)
Default nice value = 0
Higher nice value → process is “nicer” to others, uses less CPU
Lower nice value → process is more aggressive, gets more CPU
Syntax:
nice -n <nice_value> <command>
Example:
nice -n 10 sleep 1000
Starts sleep 1000 with nice value 10 (lower priority than default).
Check nice value in top or ps aux under the NI column.
2. What is "renice"?
renice changes the priority of a running process.
Can only increase priority (lower nice value) if you are root. Normal users can lower priority only.
Syntax:
renice <new_nice_value> -p <PID>
Example:
renice 15 -p 2345
Changes the nice value of PID 2345 to 15 (lower priority, more “nice”).
Multiple PIDs:
renice 5 -p 2345 -p 2389
Can also renice all processes of a user:
renice 10 -u alice
3. Checking nice values
In top:
Look at NI column → shows nice value
In ps:
ps -o pid,user,ni,cmd -p 2345

1. When a process started
a) Using ps
ps -p <PID> -o pid,lstart,etime,stat,cmd
Columns explained:
lstart → exact start date and time of the process
etime → elapsed time since the process started ([[dd-]hh:]mm:ss)
stat → process state (R, S, T, Z…)
cmd → command name

Example:

ps -p 2345 -o pid,lstart,etime,stat,cmd

Output:

  PID                  LSTART     ELAPSED   STAT CMD
 2345  Wed Mar 24 10:15:00  02:30:15  S     firefox
Process started 10:15 AM, has been running 2 hours 30 minutes.
S → sleeping (waiting), not stopped.
b) Using top
Open top:
top
Press c → see full command
Press F → select START column → shows process start time
Press M or P to sort by memory or CPU to find heavy processes
2. How long a process ran
Already shown in ps -o etime → elapsed time
In top, see TIME+ column → total CPU time used by the process

Difference:

etime → wall-clock time since start
TIME+ → CPU time actually used
3. When a process stopped and why
a) Process state in ps / top
Look at STAT column:
| STAT | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| R | Running |
| S | Sleeping (waiting for input/event) |
| T | Stopped (by signal, e.g., Ctrl+Z or kill -STOP) |
| Z | Zombie (finished but parent hasn’t collected exit status) |
| ? | No terminal associated |
Example:
ps -p 2345 -o pid,stat,cmd

Output:

PID STAT CMD
2345 T   sleep 100
T → process stopped by signal
Could be Ctrl+Z (terminal stop) or kill -STOP <PID>
b) Reason for stop
Signals explain why a process stopped:
Ctrl+C → SIGINT
Ctrl+Z → SIGTSTP
kill -STOP → SIGSTOP
kill -9 → SIGKILL → process terminated immediately
You can check exit status of a completed process:
echo $?
Only works in the same shell for foreground processes.
4. Using /proc for detailed info
For a running process:
cat /proc/<PID>/stat
Fields include:
Start time (in ticks since boot) → can convert to human-readable
Process state
Parent PID
For stopped processes, the STAT column tells reason (T = stopped, Z = zombie).

a) CPU & Process Monitoring
top            # real-time process & CPU/memory view
htop           # interactive, colorful version of top
ps aux         # snapshot of all processes
mpstat -P ALL  # CPU usage per core
b) Memory Monitoring
free -m        # free, used, cached memory in MB
vmstat 1       # memory, CPU, swap, IO stats updated every 1 second
c) Disk Monitoring
df -h          # disk space usage per partition
du -sh /path   # disk usage of a folder
iostat -x 1    # detailed disk I/O stats
iotop          # real-time I/O per process
d) Network Monitoring
