e) System Load & Uptime
uptime              # shows load average over 1, 5, 15 mins
w                   # who is logged in + what they are doing

vmstat (Virtual Memory Statistics)
Purpose: Monitor CPU, memory, swap, and system activity in real time.
Command syntax:
vmstat [delay [count]]
delay → refresh interval in seconds
count → number of updates
Example:
vmstat 2 5
Updates every 2 seconds, 5 times.
Output Columns:
procs -----------memory---------- ---swap-- -----io---- -system-- ------cpu-----
 r  b   swpd   free   buff  cache   si   so    bi    bo   in   cs us sy id wa st
Column	Meaning
r	Running processes (R state)
b	Blocked processes (waiting for I/O)
swpd	Virtual memory used (swap)
free	Free RAM
buff	Memory used as buffers
cache	Memory used as cache
si	Swap in (from disk to RAM)
so	Swap out (from RAM to disk)
bi	Blocks received from disk (read)
bo	Blocks sent to disk (write)
in	Interrupts per second
cs	Context switches per second
us	CPU time in user mode (%)
sy	CPU time in system/kernel mode (%)
id	Idle CPU (%)
wa	CPU waiting for I/O (%)
st	Stolen CPU time (virtualized environment)
Example Interpretation:
vmstat 2 5
 r  b   swpd   free  buff  cache   si   so    bi   bo   in   cs us sy id wa st
 1  0      0  4000  1000  5000    0    0     0    5  100  200 10 5 85 0 0
1 process is running, 0 blocked
4000 MB free RAM, 0 swap activity
CPU 10% user, 5% system, 85% idle → system not busy
2. iostat (Input/Output Statistics)
Purpose: Monitor disk I/O and CPU usage
Command syntax:
iostat [options] [interval [count]]
interval → refresh interval in seconds
count → number of updates
Example:
iostat -x 2 5
Extended stats (-x), refresh every 2 seconds, 5 times
Important Columns in iostat -x
Column	Meaning
Device	Disk/partition name
r/s	Reads per second
w/s	Writes per second
rkB/s	Read KB per second
wkB/s	Written KB per second
rrqm/s	Merged read requests per second
wrqm/s	Merged write requests per second
%util	% of time device was busy
await	Average wait time for I/O requests (ms)
svctm	Average service time for I/O requests (ms)
Example Interpretation:
iostat -x 2 5
Device r/s w/s rkB/s wkB/s rrqm/s wrqm/s  %util  await svctm
sda     5   10   200  500    0     2     15     2.0  0.5
Disk sda did 5 reads/sec, 10 writes/sec
200 KB read/sec, 500 KB written/sec
Disk busy 15% of the time
Average wait 2 ms, service time 0.5 ms → healthy disk usage

1. What is ulimit?
ulimit stands for user limits.
It controls how much system resources a process (or user) can use.
These limits help prevent a single user or process from overloading the system.
Types of resources controlled by ulimit:
Maximum number of open files
Maximum processes a user can create
Maximum memory usage
Maximum CPU time
Maximum file size
2. View current limits
ulimit -a
Example output:
core file size          (blocks, -c) 0
data seg size           (kbytes, -d) unlimited
scheduling priority             (-e) 0
file size               (blocks, -f) unlimited
pending signals                 (-i) 63631
max locked memory       (kbytes, -l) 64
max memory size         (kbytes, -m) unlimited
open files                      (-n) 1024
pipe size            (512 bytes, -p) 8
stack size              (kbytes, -s) 8192
cpu time               (seconds, -t) unlimited
max user processes              (-u) 63631
virtual memory          (kbytes, -v) unlimited
file locks                      (-x) unlimited
Important ones to note:
-n → max open files
-u → max user processes
-t → max CPU time
-v → max virtual memory
3. Set a new limit
Temporarily (for current shell session):
ulimit -n 2048      # max open files = 2048
ulimit -u 500       # max processes = 500
Permanently (system-wide or per user):
Edit /etc/security/limits.conf or a file in /etc/security/limits.d/
Example /etc/security/limits.conf:
alice   hard   nofile  4096
alice   soft   nofile  2048
soft limit → default limit, can be increased by user up to hard limit
hard limit → maximum allowed, requires root to increase

What is lsblk?
lsblk = list block devices
Shows all storage devices (HDDs, SSDs, USB drives) and their partitions in a tree format.
Does not show files, only block devices and how they are connected.
2. Basic Usage
lsblk
Example output:
NAME   MAJ:MIN RM  SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINT
sda      8:0    0  100G  0 disk 
├─sda1   8:1    0   50G  0 part /
├─sda2   8:2    0   49G  0 part /home
└─sda3   8:3    0    1G  0 part [SWAP]
sdb      8:16   0  200G  0 disk
└─sdb1   8:17   0  200G  0 part /data
NAME → device name (sda, sdb, sda1…)
MAJ:MIN → kernel major:minor numbers
RM → removable (1 = yes, 0 = no)
SIZE → size of disk/partition
RO → read-only (1 = yes, 0 = no)
TYPE → disk, part (partition), lvm, etc.
MOUNTPOINT → where it is mounted in the filesystem
3. Useful Options
Option	Meaning
-f	Show filesystem info (type, label, UUID)
-o	Specify columns to display
-a	Show all devices (including empty)
-J	Output in JSON (for scripts)
Examples:
lsblk -f
Output shows filesystem type (ext4, xfs, etc.), UUID, and label:
NAME   FSTYPE LABEL UUID                                 MOUNTPOINT
sda                                                       
├─sda1 ext4   root  1234-ABCD                            /
├─sda2 ext4   home  5678-EFGH                            /home
└─sda3 swap        9012-IJKL                            [SWAP]
lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,MOUNTPOINT
Custom columns → cleaner output
4. Practical Uses
Check disks and partitions
lsblk
Check mounted partitions and filesystem types
lsblk -f
Verify new disks after attaching
lsblk -a
Find which disk a mount point is on
df -hT /home
lsblk

df – Disk Free
Purpose: Shows disk space usage per filesystem/partition.
Focuses on overall space (mounted filesystems).
Basic Usage
df
Shows output like:
Filesystem     1K-blocks    Used Available Use% Mounted on
/dev/sda2      51475072 26214400 25260672  51% /
tmpfs           4042000       0  4042000   0% /dev/shm
/dev/sdb1     209715200 104857600 104857600  50% /data
Columns explained:
Filesystem → disk or partition name
1K-blocks → total size
Used → space used
Available → free space left
Use% → percentage used
Mounted on → mount point
Common Options
Option	Purpose
-h	Human-readable (MB/GB)
-T	Show filesystem type
-i	Show inode usage

