2. Display Information for a Specific Device
blkid /dev/sdb1
3. Show Only Specific Attributes
You can use -o to format output:
blkid -o value -s UUID /dev/sda1
-s specifies which attribute to show (UUID, TYPE, LABEL)
4. Using blkid in Scripts
blkid is often used in scripts to dynamically find disks:
DEVICE=$(blkid -L DATA)   # Find device by label "DATA"
5. Update the blkid Cache
RHEL maintains a cache in /etc/blkid.tab (older) or /run/blkid/blkid.tab (newer). To update:
blkid -c /dev/null
-c specifies a cache file; /dev/null forces a fresh scan

dmesg
Displays the entire kernel ring buffer (all recent messages from the kernel).
Useful for debugging hardware issues, driver problems, or boot issues.
Example output:
[    0.000000] Initializing cgroup subsys cpuset
[    0.000000] Initializing cgroup subsys cpu
[    0.003456] ACPI: RSDP 0x00000000000F0420 000024 (v02 HPQOEM)
[    1.234567] usb 1-1.2: new high-speed USB device number 3 using xhci_hcd
The numbers in brackets are timestamps (seconds since kernel start).
2. Common Options
Option	Description
-T	Show human-readable timestamps instead of seconds since boot
-k	Show only kernel messages (default)
-n <level>	Set the log level to display (0=emerg, 7=debug)
-c	Clear the kernel ring buffer after printing
--level=<level>	Filter messages by log level (err, warn, info, etc.)
Example:
dmesg -T | grep usb
Shows all USB-related kernel messages with human-readable timestamps.
3. Use Cases
Check hardware detection
New disks, USB drives, network cards, etc.
dmesg | grep -i sda
Debug driver issues
If a driver fails to load, dmesg will often show errors.
View boot messages
Especially if system boots too quickly for you to read GRUB/kernel logs.
Monitor kernel events in real-time
dmesg -w
-w makes dmesg wait and print new messages as they appear.
4. Relationship to blkid and disks
When you plug in a new disk or partition:
dmesg will log messages like:
[  12.345678] sd 2:0:0:0: [sdb] 976762584 512-byte logical blocks: (500 GB/465 GiB)
[  12.345689] sd 2:0:0:0: [sdb] Write Protect is off
[  12.345690] sd 2:0:0:0: [sdb] Attached SCSI disk
This helps confirm that the kernel detected the device before you use blkid to check UUIDs or labels.

lshw
Displays a full tree of hardware information.
Output can be very long, so it’s common to pipe it to less:
lshw | less
2. Display Specific Information
CPU info
lshw -class processor
Memory info
lshw -class memory
Disk info
lshw -class disk
Network interfaces
lshw -class network
3. Human-readable Output
lshw -short
Shows a concise overview of all hardware in a table format:
H/W path       Device      Class          Description
======================================================
/0             localhost  system         Your Server
/0/0           cpu0       processor      Intel(R) Xeon(R)
/0/1           memory     memory         16GiB System Memory
/0/100         eth0       network        Intel 82574L Gigabit
4. Output to a File
HTML (nice for documentation):
lshw -html > hardware.html
XML (for scripts or automation):
lshw -xml > hardware.xml
5. Requires Root for Full Info
Running as non-root may hide details like serial numbers, bus addresses, and firmware info.
sudo lshw
6. Common Use Cases
Inventory hardware for auditing.
Check RAM and CPU details before upgrades.
Identify network cards and disk controllers for driver installation.
