/dev/sdb2 is gone from lsblk

What is LVM?
LVM (Logical Volume Manager) is a system that lets you manage disk storage flexibly.
👉 Instead of using fixed partitions (like /dev/sda1), LVM allows:
Resize storage easily
Combine multiple disks
Take snapshots
Extend or shrink volumes
👉 Think of it like:
Traditional partition = rigid box
LVM = flexible storage pool
⚙️ Why Use LVM?
Without LVM:
Hard to resize partitions
Disk space is fixed
With LVM:
Increase/decrease size anytime
Add new disks without downtime
Better storage management
🏗️ LVM Structure (VERY IMPORTANT)
LVM has 3 layers:
1️⃣ Physical Volume (PV)
Actual disk or partition
Example:
/dev/sda
/dev/sdb1
👉 Command:
pvcreate /dev/sdb1
2️⃣ Volume Group (VG)
Pool of storage created by combining PVs
👉 Example:
Combine /dev/sdb1 + /dev/sdc1 → VG
👉 Command:
vgcreate my_vg /dev/sdb1 /dev/sdc1
👉 Think:
VG = storage pool
3️⃣ Logical Volume (LV)
Virtual partition created from VG
This is what you actually use and mount
👉 Example:
/dev/my_vg/my_lv
👉 Command:
lvcreate -L 5G -n my_lv my_vg
👉 Think:
LV = usable partition
[ Disk /dev/sdb1 ]   [ Disk /dev/sdc1 ]
          ↓                    ↓
        (PV)                (PV)
             \            /
              \          /
             [ Volume Group (VG) ]
                      ↓
        -------------------------
        |           |           |
      (LV1)       (LV2)       (LV3)

How LVM Works (Step-by-Step)
Create Physical Volume
Create Volume Group
Create Logical Volume
Format filesystem (ext4, xfs)
Mount it

Step 0: Verify Disks (Production Safety Step)
🔹 Why?
Always confirm disks before touching them — avoids data loss.
🔹 Command:
lsblk
🔹 What to check:
/dev/sdb → 30G
/dev/sdc → 40G
Ensure no important data
🧹 Step 1: Clean Disks (Very Important in Production)
🔹 Why?
Old signatures (RAID/LVM/filesystem) can break setup.
🔹 Syntax:
wipefs -a <device>
🔹 Command:
wipefs -a /dev/sdb
wipefs -a /dev/sdc
🧩 Step 2: Create Physical Volumes (PV)
🔹 Why?
LVM cannot use raw disks directly → must convert into PV.
🔹 Syntax:
pvcreate <device>
🔹 Command:
pvcreate /dev/sdb
pvcreate /dev/sdc
🔹 Verify:
pvdisplay
👉 Now both disks are LVM-ready.
🏗️ Step 3: Create Volume Group (VG)
🔹 Why?
Combine both disks into one storage pool
👉 Total space = 30 + 40 = 70 GB
🔹 Syntax:
vgcreate <vg_name> <pv1> <pv2>
🔹 Command:
vgcreate prod_vg /dev/sdb /dev/sdc
🔹 Verify:
vgdisplay
👉 Now we have a pool: prod_vg (70 GB)
📦 Step 4: Create Logical Volume (LV)
🔹 Why?
This is the usable storage (like a partition)
🔹 Syntax:
lvcreate -L <size> -n <lv_name> <vg_name>
🔹 Example (create 50GB LV):
lvcreate -L 50G -n app_lv prod_vg
👉 Remaining free space in VG = 20GB
🔹 Verify:
lvdisplay
🧾 Step 5: Create Filesystem
🔹 Why?
Without filesystem → OS cannot store files
🔹 Syntax:
mkfs.<fstype> <lv_path>
🔹 Command:
mkfs.ext4 /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
👉 You can also use XFS (common in RHEL):
mkfs.xfs /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
📂 Step 6: Create Mount Point
🔹 Why?
Directory where storage will be attached
🔹 Command:
mkdir /app_data
🔗 Step 7: Mount Logical Volume
🔹 Why?
Make storage accessible to system
🔹 Syntax:
mount <device> <mount_point>
🔹 Command:
mount /dev/prod_vg/app_lv /app_data
🔹 Verify:
df -h
🔁 Step 8: Permanent Mount (Critical in Production)
🔹 Why?
Mount should persist after reboot
🔹 Edit:
vi /etc/fstab
🔹 Entry:
/dev/prod_vg/app_lv   /app_data   ext4   defaults   0 0
👉 Test:
mount -a
⚡ Production-Level Operations
🔼 Extend Logical Volume
🔹 Why?
Application needs more storage
🔹 Syntax:
lvextend -L +<size> <lv_path>
🔹 Command:
lvextend -L +10G /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
🔹 Resize filesystem:
👉 For ext4:
resize2fs /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
👉 For XFS:
xfs_growfs /app_data
➕ Add New Disk to VG
🔹 Why?
Scale storage without downtime
pvcreate /dev/sdd
vgextend prod_vg /dev/sdd
📸 Create Snapshot (Backup Safety)
🔹 Why?
Backup before risky operations
🔹 Syntax:
lvcreate -L <size> -s -n <snap_name> <lv_path>
🔹 Command:
lvcreate -L 5G -s -n app_snap /dev/prod_vg/app_lv

What is /dev/prod_vg/app_lv?
This is the device path of your Logical Volume (LV).
prod_vg → Volume Group name
app_lv → Logical Volume name
👉 Together:
/dev/<VG_NAME>/<LV_NAME>
So:
/dev/prod_vg/app_lv
means:
“Logical volume app_lv inside volume group prod_vg”
📍 Is this path default?
✅ YES — it is automatically created by LVM
When you run:
lvcreate -L 50G -n app_lv prod_vg
👉 LVM automatically creates:
/dev/prod_vg/app_lv
You do not manually create this path.
🔗 What actually happens internally?
LVM creates device mappings in:
/dev/mapper/
Example:
/dev/mapper/prod_vg-app_lv
👉 And then creates a friendly symlink:
/dev/prod_vg/app_lv → /dev/mapper/prod_vg-app_lv
🔍 How to check this path?
1. Using lvs
lvs
Output:
LV     VG      Attr       LSize
app_lv prod_vg -wi-a----- 50.00g
2. Using lvdisplay
lvdisplay
You will see:
LV Path                /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
3. Using ls
ls /dev/prod_vg/
Output:
app_lv
🧩 Important Concept
👉 This path behaves like a real disk partition
You can:
mkfs.ext4 /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
mount /dev/prod_vg/app_lv /mnt
⚠️ Naming Rule
If you create:
vgcreate data_vg /dev/sdb
lvcreate -n backup_lv -L 10G data_vg
👉 Path becomes:
/dev/data_vg/backup_lv
So it's always:
/dev/<VG>/<LV>
🧠 Easy Way to Remember
VG = folder
LV = file
👉 So:
/dev/prod_vg/app_lv
= like:
folder/file
🚨 Production Tip
Instead of using this path in /etc/fstab, better use UUID:
blkid /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
Then:
UUID=xxxxx /app_data ext4 defaults 0 0
👉 More stable than device names

Step 1: Identify your LVM logical volume
Suppose you created:
Volume Group: prod_vg
Logical Volume: app_lv
Mount point: /app_data
The device path is:
/dev/prod_vg/app_lv
🧩 Step 2: Find the UUID (recommended)
Using UUID in /etc/fstab is safer than /dev/prod_vg/app_lv because device names may change on reboot.
blkid /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
Example output:
/dev/prod_vg/app_lv: UUID="f2d3b7e5-1234-4a5b-9a2c-1a2b3c4d5e6f" TYPE="ext4"
🧩 Step 3: Edit /etc/fstab
Open /etc/fstab:
vi /etc/fstab
Add this entry:
Option 1: Using UUID (recommended)
UUID=f2d3b7e5-1234-4a5b-9a2c-1a2b3c4d5e6f   /app_data   ext4   defaults   0 0
Option 2: Using device path (less preferred)
/dev/prod_vg/app_lv   /app_data   ext4   defaults   0 0

When deleting an LVM, you must remove in reverse order:
Logical Volume (LV) → because it depends on VG
Volume Group (VG) → because it depends on PV
Physical Volume (PV) → so disk is clean
After that, the disk can be moved to another system.
⚠️ Warning: All data will be lost. Backup anything important.
🔹 Step 0: Unmount the Logical Volume
If the LV is mounted, unmount it first.
umount /app_data
Check if unmounted:
mount | grep app_data
🔹 Step 1: Remove the Logical Volume (LV)
Why?
LV is the “usable partition”. Must be removed first.
Syntax:
lvremove <lv_path>
Command:
lvremove /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
Confirm prompt with y
Verify:
lvs
LV should be gone.
🔹 Step 2: Remove the Volume Group (VG)
Why?
VG is the “storage pool” combining PVs. Cannot remove if LV exists.
Syntax:
vgremove <vg_name>
Command:
vgremove prod_vg
Verify:
vgs
VG should be gone.
🔹 Step 3: Remove Physical Volume (PV)
Why?
PV is the disk metadata. Removing it makes disk “clean” and usable elsewhere.
Syntax:
pvremove <device>
Command:
pvremove /dev/sdb
pvremove /dev/sdc
Verify:
pvs
Disks should no longer show as PV.
🔹 Step 4: (Optional) Wipe Disk Signatures
Extra safe in production before giving disk to another PC:
wipefs -a /dev/sdb
wipefs -a /dev/sdc
Or zero out MBR/GPT if required:
dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdb bs=512 count=2048
dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sdc bs=512 count=2048
Now the disks are completely clean and can be used anywhere.
🔹 Step 5: Verification
lsblk
Disks /dev/sdb and /dev/sdc appear as raw, no partitions, no LVM.

Scenario
Volume Group: prod_vg
Logical Volume: app_lv
Current LV size: 50 GB
Volume Group free space: 20 GB
Mount point: /app_data
Filesystem type: ext4
Goal: Extend LV by 10 GB, so new total size = 60 GB.
🔹 Step 0: Check Current Status
Before doing anything, always check current LV, VG, PV:
lvs
vgs
pvs
df -h | grep /app_data
Output might look like:
LV     VG      LSize  ...
app_lv prod_vg 50.00g
vgs shows free PE (Physical Extents)
df -h shows current filesystem usage
✅ This is critical in production — never extend blindly.
🔹 Step 1: Extend the Logical Volume (LV)
Why?
LV is the virtual “partition”. Must extend before filesystem.
Syntax:
lvextend -L +<size> <lv_path>
Command:
lvextend -L +10G /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
+10G → adds 10 GB to existing LV
LV path: /dev/prod_vg/app_lv (automatically created by LVM)
🔹 Step 2: Extend the Filesystem
After LV extension, the filesystem still thinks it’s 50 GB.
Must resize filesystem to use extra space.
🔹 For ext4:
resize2fs /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
Automatically expands filesystem to fill LV
🔹 For XFS (default in RHEL 7+):
xfs_growfs /app_data
XFS grows while mounted → production-friendly
🔹 Step 3: Verify
df -h | grep /app_data
lvs
Filesystem now shows 60 GB total space
LV shows 60 GB size
Application can use new space immediately
🔹 Step 4: Optional — Extend LV Using New Disk
If the VG has no free space, add a disk first:
pvcreate /dev/sdd
vgextend prod_vg /dev/sdd
Then extend LV as above.
This is real production scenario: adding new storage without downtime
🔹 Production Tips
Always check VG free space before extending:
vgs
For ext4, you can extend while mounted, but safer to unmount in critical apps.
For XFS, filesystem must be mounted.
Use lvdisplay and df -h to verify each step.

We’ll assume:

Existing VG: prod_vg
LV: app_lv
Mount point: /app_data
New disk: /dev/sdd (40 GB)
Filesystem type: ext4
🧠 Step 0: Check Current Status
vgs
lvs
pvs
df -h | grep /app_data
Check VG free space (Free PE)
Check LV size and mount point usage

Suppose VG shows 0 free space, LV is 50 GB, and filesystem is ext4.

🔹 Step 1: Prepare the New Disk
Why?

Disk must be LVM-ready before adding to VG.

Command:
pvcreate /dev/sdd
Writes LVM metadata on /dev/sdd
Verify:
pvs

You should see /dev/sdd as LVM PV, unassigned to any VG yet.

🔹 Step 2: Extend the Volume Group (VG)
Why?

VG is the storage pool; must include new disk to extend LV.

Command:
vgextend prod_vg /dev/sdd
Adds new disk to prod_vg
Verify:
vgs
Free PE should now include the space from /dev/sdd (≈ 40 GB)
🔹 Step 3: Extend the Logical Volume (LV)
Why?

Now that VG has free space, we can extend LV.

Command (increase by 30 GB from new disk):
lvextend -L +30G /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
+30G → add 30 GB to existing LV
LV path: /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
Verify:
lvs
🔹 Step 4: Resize the Filesystem

After LV extension, filesystem still thinks it’s old size. Must resize.

For ext4:
resize2fs /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
For XFS:
xfs_growfs /app_data
Filesystem now uses full LV size
No data lost, can do online if filesystem supports it (XFS/ext4 mounted)
🔹 Step 5: Verify Everything
df -h | grep /app_data
lvs
vgs
pvs
df -h → filesystem shows new size (50 + 30 = 80 GB)
lvs → LV shows 80 GB
vgs → Free PE reduced accordingly
pvs → shows /dev/sdd as part of VG

We’ll assume:
Volume Group: prod_vg
Logical Volume: app_lv
Mount point: /app_data
Filesystem type: ext4
Current LV size: 50 GB
Goal: shrink LV to 30 GB
⚠️ Important Safety Notes
Always backup data before shrinking.
Do not shrink a mounted LV unless filesystem supports online shrink (ext4 does not).
For XFS — cannot shrink XFS. Only ext4/ext3 can be shrunk.
🔹 Step 0: Check Current Status
df -h | grep /app_data
lvs
vgs
Check used space — must be less than target size.
Example: app_lv 50 GB, only 20 GB used → safe to shrink to 30 GB.
🔹 Step 1: Unmount the Logical Volume
umount /app_data
Verify:
mount | grep /app_data
Must not appear → LV is unmounted.
🔹 Step 2: Check and Shrink the Filesystem
Shrink filesystem before shrinking LV, otherwise you can corrupt data.
2a: Check filesystem
e2fsck -f /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
Forces check for consistency
2b: Shrink filesystem
resize2fs /dev/prod_vg/app_lv 30G
30G → new size for filesystem
Filesystem shrunk safely
🔹 Step 3: Shrink the Logical Volume
lvreduce -L 30G /dev/prod_vg/app_lv
Confirms shrink if prompted
30G → new LV size
Must match or be slightly larger than filesystem
🔹 Step 4: Mount the Logical Volume
mount /dev/prod_vg/app_lv /app_data
Verify:
df -h | grep /app_data
lvs
LV and filesystem now 30 GB

lvs – Logical Volume Summary
✅ Purpose
Shows all Logical Volumes (LVs) on the system in a compact table format
Quick way to see LV name, VG, size, attributes
🔹 Syntax
lvs [options]
🔹 Example
lvs
🔹 Sample Output
LV      VG       Attr       LSize   Pool Origin Data%  Meta%  Move Log Cpy%Sync Convert
app_lv  prod_vg  -wi-ao---- 50.00g
🔹 What It Tells
LV → Logical Volume name
VG → Volume Group the LV belongs to
Attr → Attributes (writable, snapshot, etc.)
LSize → LV size
Quick way to see all logical volumes and their size.
🧠 2️⃣ vgs – Volume Group Summary
✅ Purpose
Shows all Volume Groups (VGs)
Quick overview of total size, free space, number of LVs/PVs
🔹 Syntax
vgs [options]
🔹 Example
vgs
🔹 Sample Output
VG       #PV #LV #SN Attr   VSize   VFree
prod_vg   2   1   0 wz--n- 70.00g 20.00g
🔹 What It Tells
#PV → Number of physical volumes
#LV → Number of logical volumes
VSize → Total size of VG
