What Are Targets in RHEL (Systemd)?
Targets = “modern runlevels” in systemd-based Linux (RHEL 7/8/9)
They define a state of the system, telling which services should start at boot or when switching modes.
Think of a target as a preset system mode, not just “restart” — it tells Linux: “Start these services and stop everything else.”

🧠 2️⃣ Examples of Targets
Target Name       What It Means                             Equivalent Runlevel
poweroff.target   Shut down system                          0
reboot.target     Reboot system                              6
rescue.target     Single-user maintenance mode               1
emergency.target  Minimal mode for critical repair           N/A
multi-user.target Multi-user CLI system (network, no GUI)   3
graphical.target  Multi-user system with GUI                 5

🧠 3️⃣ Why Targets Exist
Control Services → Only the services needed for that target start. Example: rescue.target → only root and essential services start.
Flexible Boot Modes → Boot into CLI, GUI, single-user, or emergency mode.
Replace old runlevels → Use descriptive names instead of numbers (0-6).
Switch Mode Without Reboot → Move the system into another mode while running.

🧠 4️⃣ How Targets Are Used
🔹 Check Current Target
systemctl get-default
Example output: graphical.target

🔹 Change Default Target (Permanent)
sudo systemctl set-default multi-user.target
System will boot into CLI mode instead of GUI

🔹 Switch Target Temporarily (Current Session)
sudo systemctl isolate rescue.target
System switches immediately to single-user mode (does not persist after reboot)

🔹 Reboot or Power Off Using Target
sudo systemctl isolate reboot.target
sudo systemctl isolate poweroff.target
System switches into reboot/shutdown mode


What SELinux is SELinux is a mandatory access control (MAC) system. That means it controls who (or what process) can access which files, ports, or resources, even if traditional permissions would allow it. Think of it like this: Normal Linux permissions = “user/group can read/write/execute” SELinux = “even if allowed, should this action really happen?”

Enforcing Mode
SELinux policy is actively enforced.
Unauthorized access is blocked.
Violations are logged in /var/log/audit/audit.log.
2. Permissive Mode
SELinux policy is not enforced.
Access that would normally be blocked is allowed, but violations are still logged.
Useful for testing or debugging policies.
3. Disabled Mode
SELinux is completely turned off.
No policies are enforced, and nothing is logged.
How to Check SELinux Mode
Using getenforce
getenforce
Outputs: Enforcing, Permissive, or Disabled.
Using sestatus
sestatus
Shows detailed info: current mode, policy loaded, and booleans.
1. Temporarily Change SELinux Mode
This change doesn’t persist after a reboot.
Use the setenforce command:
sudo setenforce 1   # Switch to Enforcing
sudo setenforce 0   # Switch to Permissive
You can verify with:
getenforce
2. Permanently Change SELinux Mode
Edit the SELinux configuration file:
sudo nano /etc/selinux/config
Look for the line:
SELINUX=enforcing
Change it to one of the following:
SELINUX=enforcing   # Enforcing mode
SELINUX=permissive  # Permissive mode
SELINUX=disabled    # Disabled mode
Save the file and reboot your system for changes to take effect.

A context is like a “security label” assigned to every file, process, and resource. It tells SELinux who can do what based on the policy.
Structure of an SELinux Context
A context has four main parts:
user:role:type:level
User – SELinux user identity (not the Linux username)
Role – Defines what roles a user can assume (usually object_r for files)
Type – The most important part; defines access rules for processes/files (httpd_t, ssh_t, etc.)
Level – Optional; used in MLS (Multi-Level Security) systems (s0:c0,c1)
Example:
system_u:object_r:httpd_sys_content_t:s0
system_u → SELinux user
object_r → Role
httpd_sys_content_t → Type (label for web content)
s0 → Security level
How to View SELinux Context
For files:
ls -Z /path/to/file
For processes:
ps -eZ | grep process_name
Example: check context of Apache:
ps -eZ | grep httpd
SELinux uses these contexts to allow or deny access. Even if Linux file permissions allow an action, SELinux will block it if the context rules don’t match.

1. SELinux User
This is not your Linux username.
SELinux comes with predefined users, e.g.:
system_u – for system processes
unconfined_u – for normal users without restrictions
staff_u – for staff-type users
You usually select one of these predefined users; you rarely create your own unless you are writing a custom policy.
2. Role
Roles define what the SELinux user is allowed to do.
Common roles:
object_r – for files/objects
system_r – for system daemons
staff_r – for staff users
Roles are mostly predefined in SELinux policy. You pick a valid one; you don’t invent new roles unless you’re writing custom SELinux policies.
3. Type
This is the most important part of the context. It’s what SELinux uses to allow or deny actions.
Examples:
httpd_t – Apache process
httpd_sys_content_t – web files
ssh_t – SSH process
You usually use predefined types from the SELinux policy.
If your application or service is custom, you can create a new type, but this requires writing a custom policy module.
4. Level
Only used in MLS (Multi-Level Security) systems.
Standard Linux setups usually just have s0.
Predefined; you normally don’t create your own.

✅ Summary:

Most SELinux contexts are predefined.

What is Type Enforcement (TE)?
Type Enforcement (TE) is the main access control mechanism in SELinux.
In TE, everything (files, processes, ports, etc.) is assigned a “type”.
Policies then define which types can interact with which other types, and how.

Think of it like a traffic control system:

Each process and resource has a type (like a traffic badge).
TE rules are the traffic rules: they say, “process type A can read file type B, but cannot execute it.”
How It Works
Assign types
Files, directories, processes, and ports all get a type.
Examples:
Process: httpd_t (Apache server)
File: httpd_sys_content_t (web files)
Define rules
Rules are written in the SELinux policy.
Example:
Allow httpd_t processes to read httpd_sys_content_t files.
Deny httpd_t from writing /etc/passwd.
Enforce access
When a process tries to access a resource, SELinux checks the type of the process and the type of the resource against the rules.
Access is allowed or denied based on the policy.
Example
Process type: httpd_t (Apache)
File type: httpd_sys_content_t (web content)

Policy rule:
allow httpd_t httpd_sys_content_t:dir { read write execute };
Meaning: Apache can read/write/execute files labeled as web content.

1. Understand the Components
Take this example:
allow httpd_t httpd_sys_content_t:dir { read write execute };
allow → This is the permission type, meaning “grant access.”
httpd_t → Source type (the process, e.g., Apache).
httpd_sys_content_t → Target type (the resource, e.g., files/directories).
dir → The class of object (could be file, dir, port, etc.).
{ read write execute } → Permissions granted for that class.
2. Write a Custom Policy Module
Create a .te file
Example: myhttpd.te
module myhttpd 1.0;
require {
    type httpd_t;
    type httpd_sys_content_t;
    class dir { read write execute };
}
# Allow httpd_t to read/write/execute directories labeled httpd_sys_content_t
allow httpd_t httpd_sys_content_t:dir { read write execute };
module myhttpd 1.0; → Names your policy module
require { ... } → Declares the types and classes you will reference
allow ... → The actual rule
Compile the policy module
checkmodule -M -m -o myhttpd.mod myhttpd.te
-M -m → Generate a module from the TE file
-o myhttpd.mod → Output file
Package it into a loadable module
semodule_package -o myhttpd.pp -m myhttpd.mod
.pp file → SELinux policy package
Install the module
sudo semodule -i myhttpd.pp
This loads the rule into SELinux.
Changes are persistent.
Verify the module
semodule -l | grep myhttpd
Confirms your policy module is installed.
✅ Tips:
Use audit2allow if SELinux logs show blocked access — it can help generate TE rules automatically.
Always test in permissive mode first to avoid locking your system.

What SELinux Booleans Do
SELinux policies are strict by default.
Booleans let you adjust behavior for specific use cases.
Example: Allowing Apache (httpd_t) to serve user home directories without writing a new policy.
2. How to View Booleans
getsebool -a
Lists all SELinux booleans and their current state (on or off).
Example snippet:
allow_httpd_anon_write --> off
httpd_enable_homedirs --> on
allow_httpd_anon_write → if turned on, Apache can write to anonymous directories
httpd_enable_homedirs → allows serving user home directories
3. How to Change Booleans
Temporarily (until next reboot)
sudo setsebool httpd_enable_homedirs on
sudo setsebool httpd_enable_homedirs off
Permanently
sudo setsebool -P httpd_enable_homedirs on
-P flag makes it persistent across reboots
4. Examples
Allow Apache to connect to the network:
sudo setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect on
Allow MySQL to read/write home directories:
sudo setsebool -P mysql_connect_home_dirs on
✅ Key Points:
Booleans are predefined switches in SELinux policies.
They let you adjust behavior without writing custom modules.
Always check with getsebool -a before changing.

Where SELinux Logs Are Stored
Main location:
/var/log/audit/audit.log
Sometimes, denied access messages also appear in:
/var/log/messages
On systems without auditd, SELinux messages may go to the system journal:
journalctl | grep SELinux
2. Reading SELinux Logs
Example: see recent denied access attempts:
ausearch -m avc -ts today
avc = Access Vector Cache messages (denials)
-ts today = filter from today
Another way:
grep "AVC" /var/log/audit/audit.log
3. Understanding an AVC Denial Message
Example log line:
type=AVC msg=audit(1678372000.123:456): avc:  denied  { write } for  pid=1234 comm="httpd" name="index.html" dev="sda1" ino=5678 scontext=system_u:system_r:httpd_t:s0 tcontext=system_u:object_r:httpd_sys_content_t:s0 tclass=file
Breakdown:
pid=1234 → Process ID
comm="httpd" → Command / process name
scontext=system_u:system_r:httpd_t:s0 → Source context (process)
tcontext=system_u:object_r:httpd_sys_content_t:s0 → Target context (file)
{ write } → Denied action
tclass=file → Class of object
4. Using audit2allow to Generate Policy
SELinux provides a tool to turn AVC denials into allow rules:
ausearch -m avc -ts today | audit2allow -M mypolicy
This generates a policy module mypolicy.pp you can install:
sudo semodule -i mypolicy.pp
Always review rules before applying to avoid accidentally giving too much access.
✅ Key Points
Audit logs = your main window into SELinux activity
Denials appear as AVC messages
Use ausearch + audit2allow to analyze and create rules safely



Scenario: Root password is forgotten, and you need to regain access.
Step 1: Reboot the system
If the system is running, reboot it:
reboot
If you are already at the login prompt, you may need to physically access the server console or use the hypervisor/remote console.
Why:
We need to boot into rescue or emergency mode to change the password.
Step 2: Interrupt GRUB at boot
During boot, when the GRUB menu appears:
Highlight the default RHEL kernel.
Press e to edit the boot parameters.
Why:
We’re going to boot into single-user mode or emergency mode to reset the password.
Step 3: Edit the boot parameters
Find the line starting with:
linux16 /vmlinuz-<version> ...
or
linux /vmlinuz-<version> ...
At the end of the line, append one of these:
For RHEL 7/8:
rd.break
(Alternative older method: single or 1)
Meaning:
rd.break → Breaks before the root filesystem is mounted in read-write mode.
Allows emergency shell to reset password.
Step 4: Boot into emergency shell
Press Ctrl + X to boot with the modified parameters.
You’ll see a prompt like:
switch_root:/#
Step 5: Remount the root filesystem in read/write mode
By default, the filesystem is read-only.
Command:
mount -o remount,rw /sysroot
Meaning:
mount → remount filesystem
-o remount,rw → remount read/write
/sysroot → root filesystem in emergency mode
Step 6: Chroot into the system
Command:
chroot /sysroot
Meaning:
chroot → Change root to /sysroot
Gives normal system environment for changing passwords.
Step 7: Reset the root password
Command:
passwd root
You’ll be prompted to enter a new password twice.
Example:
New password: <type new password>
Retype new password: <confirm>
Meaning:
passwd → changes the password for the given user (here root)
Updates /etc/shadow with new hash.
Step 8: Relabel SELinux context (Important)
In RHEL, booting with rd.break bypasses SELinux, so we must relabel files.
Command:
touch /.autorelabel
Meaning:
Forces SELinux to relabel filesystem on next boot.
Prevents SELinux denial errors after changing password.
Step 9: Exit chroot and reboot
Exit chroot:
exit
Remount root (optional):
mount -o remount,ro /sysroot
Continue boot:
exit
System will boot normally. SELinux will relabel in background (may take some minutes).
Step 10: Test the new password
At login prompt, enter:
root
<new password>
If successful, root access is restored.

What is chroot?
chroot stands for “change root”.
It is a Linux command that changes the apparent root directory (/) for a process and its children.
After running chroot /newroot, the process cannot see anything outside /newroot.
It effectively isolates the environment, creating a “mini Linux system” inside a directory.
Think of it as giving a process its own “sandboxed root filesystem.”
Why it’s used
System recovery / rescue
When you boot RHEL in emergency mode (rd.break), the root filesystem is not fully mounted.
chroot /sysroot allows you to operate as if you booted normally, so you can:
Reset root password
Reinstall bootloader
Fix configuration files
Testing / isolation
Developers can test software in a chroot environment without affecting the main system.
Package building
Some Linux distributions use chroot to build packages in a clean environment.
Basic Syntax
chroot [OPTION] NEWROOT [COMMAND]
NEWROOT → directory to act as new /
[COMMAND] → optional command to run inside chroot (default is a shell)
Example 1: Enter emergency root
chroot /sysroot
/sysroot is mounted root filesystem from emergency mode
After running this, your shell behaves as if /sysroot is /
You can now run passwd root or yum update kernel as if the system is booted normally.
Example 2: Run a command inside chroot
chroot /mnt /bin/bash
Starts a bash shell with /mnt as /
All commands run inside are limited to /mnt

GRUB Password in RHEL
Purpose
Protect GRUB menu from unauthorized edits.
Prevent someone from:
Booting into single-user/emergency mode.
Changing kernel parameters.
Accessing root without logging in.
RHEL uses GRUB 2. Passwords are stored in hashed form in /boot/grub2/user.cfg (BIOS) or /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg (UEFI).
Step 1: Generate a GRUB password hash
Use grub2-setpassword command:
grub2-setpassword
You’ll be prompted:
Enter new password:
Confirm new password:
Meaning:
grub2-setpassword → prompts for a password and generates hashed password for GRUB 2.
The hash is stored in /boot/grub2/user.cfg.
Important: This is secure, the password is not stored in plain text.
Alternative: Manual hash generation
grub2-mkpasswd-pbkdf2
Prompts for password
Outputs something like:
PBKDF2 hash of your password is grub.pbkdf2.sha512.10000.abcdef1234567890...
You can use this hash directly in /etc/grub.d/40_custom for manual setup.
Step 2: Update GRUB configuration
BIOS-based system
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
UEFI-based system
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg
Meaning:
grub2-mkconfig → generates the actual grub.cfg file from scripts in /etc/grub.d and /etc/default/grub.
-o <file> → output file for GRUB to use at boot.
This ensures the password protection is applied at boot.
Step 3: Test GRUB password
Reboot the system:
reboot
At GRUB menu, try:
Press e to edit a kernel entry
It should prompt for the password
Meaning:
If password is correct → GRUB allows booting
If wrong → access denied
Step 4: Optional - Manual setup using 40_custom
If you want to manually add the password:
Edit file:
vi /etc/grub.d/40_custom
Add:
set superusers="root"
password_pbkdf2 root <paste-your-hash-here>
Regenerate GRUB config:
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg   # BIOS
# or
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg   # UEFI
Step 5: Important Production Notes
Always backup grub.cfg before making changes:
cp /boot/grub2/grub.cfg /boot/grub2/grub.cfg.bak
Verify kernel entries before rebooting.
Misconfiguring GRUB can make system unbootable.
UEFI vs BIOS
BIOS → /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
UEFI → /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg
Password types
password_pbkdf2 → hashed, secure, recommended
password → plain text, insecure (avoid in production)
Step 6: Reset GRUB password if forgotten
If you forget the GRUB password:
Boot using RHEL installation media (rescue mode).
Mount root filesystem:
mount /dev/sda1 /mnt
Chroot into the system:
chroot /mnt
Remove or regenerate /boot/grub2/user.cfg:
rm /boot/grub2/user.cfg
grub2-setpassword
Regenerate GRUB config:
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
Reboot and test.
This is the safe emergency method to reset GRUB password in production.

Step 1: Obtain RHEL Installation Media
Download the RHEL ISO from the Red Hat Customer Portal.
Make sure the ISO matches the same version/minor release as your installed system.
Make it bootable:

USB drive (pendrive):
# On Linux, assuming ISO is rhel.iso and USB is /dev/sdb
sudo dd if=rhel.iso of=/dev/sdb bs=4M status=progress && sync
dd → writes the ISO to the USB
bs=4M → block size, faster write
sync → ensures all data is written

DVD: burn ISO using your preferred software.
Production Tip: Bootable media must be physically connected to the server before reboot.

Step 2: Boot from Installation Media
Reboot the server.
Enter BIOS/UEFI boot menu:
Keys vary: F12, F10, Esc, Del
Select USB or DVD drive as boot device.
Meaning:
The system temporarily boots from external media instead of your hard disk.
This is essential because we cannot log in normally when root password is forgotten.
✅ No Linux commands run here yet; this is BIOS/boot-level operation.

Step 3: Select “Troubleshooting”
Once the RHEL installation menu appears:
Highlight Troubleshooting (bottom of menu)
Press Enter
This gives access to rescue and recovery options.

Step 4: Choose “Rescue a Red Hat Enterprise Linux system”
Select Rescue a RHEL system → Enter
The system will start a minimal rescue environment.

Step 5: Mount your Linux installation
Prompt appears:
1) Continue with mounting your system under /mnt/sysimage
2) Skip mounting your system
Choose 1 → mounts your system under /mnt/sysimage
Meaning:
/mnt/sysimage is now your root filesystem mount point.
You can now access /etc/shadow, /boot/grub2, /etc/grub.d from the live rescue environment.
✅ This is the first point where commands actually interact with the installation media:
The rescue environment reads your mounted partitions from the hard disk while booting from the media.

Step 5: Mount Your Linux Installation — Commands
When you choose option 1 in rescue mode, the system automatically mounts your installed root filesystem under /mnt/sysimage. But sometimes you may need to do it manually (or verify it). Here’s how:

1. Identify your root partition
Before mounting, find out which disk/partition contains your root filesystem:
lsblk -f
Example output:
NAME   FSTYPE LABEL UUID                                 MOUNTPOINT
sda
├─sda1 ext4         a1b2c3d4-5678-90ef-1234-567890abcdef /boot
├─sda2 xfs          1234abcd-5678-90ef-1234-abcdef567890 /
└─sda3 swap         abcdef12-3456-7890-abcd-ef1234567890 [SWAP]

In this example:
/dev/sda2 is root /
/dev/sda1 is /boot

2. Mount root filesystem manually (optional)
If the rescue environment didn’t automatically mount:
mount /dev/sda2 /mnt/sysimage
mount → attaches a filesystem to a directory.
/dev/sda2 → root partition of your installed system.
/mnt/sysimage → mount point provided by rescue environment.

3. Mount /boot (if separate partition)
If /boot is on a separate partition (/dev/sda1 in example):
mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/sysimage/boot
Ensures GRUB files and kernel images are accessible.

4. Verify mounts
ls /mnt/sysimage
Should show directories like: etc, boot, home, var
df -h /mnt/sysimage
Shows which partition is mounted and confirms size/usage.

Step 6: Enter Shell
chroot /mnt/sysimage
Changes root to your installed system so you can run commands as if booted normally.

Step 7: Reset Password or Repair GRUB
passwd root                       # Reset root password
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg   # Regenerate GRUB
fsck -f /dev/sda1                 # Check filesystem (if needed)
Important: /dev/sda1 should be your system partition, not the USB/installation media. Use lsblk to confirm.

Step 8: Exit and Reboot
exit       # Exit chroot
reboot    # Reboot system
Remove USB/DVD so the system boots from disk.

✅ Key Clarifications About Installation Media
No Linux commands run while booting the ISO — the media itself provides a temporary Linux environment.
Commands start after rescue environment loads.
Mounting partitions (/mnt/sysimage) is how the live environment accesses your system.
Always confirm which disk is your system root vs the USB (lsblk) — /dev/sda1 could sometimes be the pendrive.

Step 1: Install vsftpd
sudo yum install vsftpd -y
Explanation:
yum install vsftpd → installs the Very Secure FTP Daemon package.
-y → automatically confirms installation.
Production tip: Always install from RHEL official repositories for security.

Step 2: Start and Enable vsftpd Service
sudo systemctl start vsftpd
sudo systemctl enable vsftpd
sudo systemctl status vsftpd
Explanation:
systemctl start vsftpd → starts the FTP service immediately.
systemctl enable vsftpd → makes it auto-start on boot.
systemctl status vsftpd → checks service status.

Step 3: Configure Firewall
Allow FTP ports:
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ftp
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
Explanation:
--add-service=ftp → opens port 21 for FTP traffic.
--reload → applies changes.
Production note: For passive FTP, additional ports may need opening (e.g., 30000–31000).

Step 4: Backup and Edit vsftpd Configuration
Backup original config:
sudo cp /etc/vsftpd/vsftpd.conf /etc/vsftpd/vsftpd.conf.bak
Edit:
sudo vi /etc/vsftpd/vsftpd.conf
Key parameters to set:
Parameter	Value	Explanation
anonymous_enable	NO	Disable anonymous FTP for security
local_enable	YES	Allow local users to login
write_enable	YES	Allow uploading/modifying files
chroot_local_user	YES	Restrict users to their home directories
pasv_min_port	30000	For passive FTP range
pasv_max_port	31000	For passive FTP range
allow_writeable_chroot=YES

Step 5: Restart vsftpd Service
sudo systemctl restart vsftpd
Reloads the configuration changes.

Step 6: Create FTP Users
sudo useradd ftpuser
sudo passwd ftpuser
Creates a dedicated FTP user (ftpuser).
Production tip: Use restricted directories and avoid giving FTP users root access.
Optional: Restrict FTP user to their home directory:
sudo mkdir -p /home/ftpuser/ftp
sudo chown ftpuser:ftpuser /home/ftpuser/ftp

Step 7: SELinux Considerations (if enforcing)
Check SELinux status:
sestatus
If enforcing, allow FTP write:
sudo setsebool -P ftp_home_dir on
Explanation:
ftp_home_dir on → allows users to upload files to their home directory.

Step 8: Test FTP Server
From a client or localhost:
ftp <server-ip>
Login with ftpuser credentials
Test upload/download files:
put testfile.txt
get testfile.txt

Step 9: Optional – Enable Passive FTP for Firewalls/NAT
In /etc/vsftpd/vsftpd.conf:
pasv_enable=YES
pasv_min_port=30000
pasv_max_port=31000
Then open ports in firewall:
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=30000-31000/tcp
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

